const study = studies.find((entry) => entry.slug === "earth-2026-11-14");
if (!study) throw new Error("Missing Earth study metadata");
---

<StudyLayout study={study}>
  <p>
    Follow-up · 16 November 2026: the
    <a href="/lab/studies/rock-atmospheres-2026-11-15"
      >accepted rock-atmosphere study</a
    >
    selects Impact with Cirrus and atmospheric heat as placeholder Earth for
    Practice. Earth exploration concludes for now. This study remains stopped;
    the placeholder does not accept its earlier Earth or habitation designs.
  </p>

  <section aria-label="Study outcome">
    <h2>Stopped · 15 November 2026</h2>
    <p>
      This cycle was ended because the work started over-correcting and looping
      on irrelevant details, and the study was declared failed. Why the loop
      formed was not established. No inhabited planet design was accepted for
      Void Drift. The earlier appreciation of the research and learning remains
      part of the record; it does not change that outcome.
    </p>
    <p>
      The retained finding is that improving isolated planet images did not
      establish how an inhabited world with an atmosphere belongs in the game.
      Repeated oversteering from tentative ideas was identified, including the
      interrupted 02.10 attempt. The study ends in exploration; readiness and
      implementation scope were not reached.
    </p>
    <p>
      References, sketches and the 02.8–02.9 reasoning remain below. Surface,
      atmosphere, habitation and Earth’s identity remain unresolved. Temporary
      rendering components have been retired. The closure evidence is recorded
      in <code>docs/loop-evidence/2026-11-15-earth-reference-research.md</code>.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2 id="01">01 / An inhabited world at flight scale</h2>
    <p>
      Earth will be the first inhabited Earth-like planet in Practice, beside
      Mercury and Venus. Its land, water, clouds, air and night-side habitation
      should make it feel alive. Their treatment should also inform other
      Earth-like worlds.
    </p>
    <p>
      This prototype must be readily recognizable as Earth at flight scale. Void
      Drift’s visual language leads: familiar geography supplies identity, with
      freedom to simplify, exaggerate and compose. The open question is how much
      detail Earth needs, and how its surface, atmosphere and inhabited night
      combine into a convincing world.
    </p>
    <p>
      The study settles the visual treatment of one Earth prototype for
      Practice. Size, simplification and composition remain open. Mercury,
      Venus, the G7 Sun, controls, gravity and contact rules stay fixed.
      Placement, motion and mass are later delivery choices; landing and
      atmospheric damage are outside this visual cycle.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        What visual treatment makes an inhabited Earth-like planet feel like a
        convincing world beside Mercury and Venus at flight scale, using Earth
        as the first prototype?
      </li>
      <li>
        How do we depict Earth in Void Drift’s visual language so it is readily
        recognizable at flight scale and belongs beside Mercury and Venus?
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      <strong>Scope amended · 15 November 2026.</strong>
      <a href="#02-8">Reasoning and retained findings in 02.8</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Historical question test · 14 November 2026, reframed:</strong>
      One subject — Pass; Grounded — Pass with findings; Consequence — Pass with
      findings. Corrections and the 15 November amendment have not been
      retested.
      <a href="/lab/reviews/earth-2026-11-14">Full result and earlier rubric</a
      >.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2 id="02">02 / Finding a convincing Earth</h2>
    <details>
      <summary id="02-1">
        02.1 / The atmosphere belongs to the whole globe
      </summary>
      <p>
        A retains the mapped surface, observed cloud pattern and inhabited
        night. B adds blue haze that strengthens towards the limb, a softly
        fading lit edge and a restrained warm transition into darkness. The
        proposal is that these connected cues can suggest an atmosphere with
        depth while keeping the land and ocean recognizable. The apparent
        thickness and colour are deliberately composed for the game, not a
        physical scattering simulation.
      </p>
      <figure>
        <img
          class="comparison"
          src="/studies/earth-2026-11-14/02-1-atmosphere.png"
          width="1056"
          height="930"
          alt="Retained 02.1 comparison: Earth without and with blue atmosphere, enlarged and beside Mercury and Venus, followed by three longitudes."
        >
        <figcaption>
          02.1 retained capture, 14 November 2026, 1280px viewport. Conditions
          below. The static image preserves the exact comparison independently
          of later rendering changes.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        Both use identical sources, cloud opacity, night-light treatment, sphere
        projection and the shared upper-left planetary light. The small rows use
        production Mercury and Venus materials at their current radii, alongside
        an Earth fixture of r42u. The paired views are 3.2, 0.7 and 0.4 px/u;
        the lower three longitudes are 1.8 px/u. Only the atmospheric treatment
        changes between A and B. Fixed images, no random generation, orbit or
        elapsed-time animation; the three longitudes are separate views, not a
        selected rotation speed.
      </p>
      <p>
        Day surface: NASA Blue Marble Next Generation, July 2004 base map.
        Clouds: NASA’s 2002 Blue Marble cloud composite, retrieved from a
        <a
          href="https://phys23p.sl.psu.edu/phys_anim/astro/parallax/cloud_combined_2048.jpg"
          >Penn State hosted copy</a
        >
        because the legacy NASA asset host was unavailable. Night: Black Marble
        2016. These are separate historical observations, not one simultaneous
        Earth. All three retain full global map coverage and share the same
        projection; cloud brightness supplies illustrative opacity, and night
        lights are extracted from the colour map, suppressed in daylight and
        attenuated beneath cloud. No future geography or technology has been
        added.
      </p>
      <p>
        Imagery credits: NASA Earth Observatory / Reto Stöckli (Blue Marble land
        and clouds); NASA Earth Observatory / Joshua Stevens, using Suomi NPP
        VIIRS data from Miguel Román, NASA GSFC (Black Marble).
        <a
          href="https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-observatory/blue-marble-next-generation/base-map/"
          >Day source</a
        >,
        <a
          href="https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-observatory/the-blue-marble-true-color-global-imagery-at-1km-resolution/"
          >cloud source and method</a
        >,
        <a
          href="https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-observatory/earth-at-night/maps/"
          >night source</a
        >.
        <a href="/studies/earth-2026-11-14/sources.json"
          >Asset provenance and treatment</a
        >.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Retained finding:</strong>
        the comparison was a useful design step, but the Earth surface did not
        yet feel convincing enough to judge the fit of its atmosphere. Assessing
        the atmosphere and its effects against an unsettled surface confounds
        the judgment. Neither A nor B is an accepted atmosphere or surface.
        Establish a convincing planetary body before returning to the
        atmospheric treatment.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two paths emerged: use the established Mercury-like rock material to
        investigate atmosphere on a familiar body, or establish inhabited
        Earth’s material through astronomical references. The former could
        isolate an atmospheric cue, but would not settle its fit to oceans,
        weather and city lights. 02.2 first seeks an appearance target in intact
        Earth portraits. The lesson belongs to this comparison; it is not a
        claim that atmosphere can always be separated from a planet’s
        appearance.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Adjacent gameplay question:</strong>
        could a close atmospheric pass cause heat damage, perhaps in a band 1–5u
        above the body? This is an open proposal, not accepted tuning or part of
        02.2. Whether danger depends on speed, depth and time in the atmosphere,
        and how its boundary is made readable, needs a separate encounter
        investigation. Visible haze does not currently define a damage zone;
        existing contact rules remain fixed.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary id="02-2">
        02.2 / Choose an appearance before reconstructing it
      </summary>
      <p>
        <strong>Retained finding from the withdrawn rendering proposal:</strong>
        deeper colour felt candy-like and reminiscent of limited-palette pixel
        art. The surface problem from 02.1 remained. Changing colour, clouds and
        illumination together did not establish which assumption was wrong;
        further correction from that example would lack a shared visual target.
        The proposal and its dedicated renderer are withdrawn. This replacement
        compares source portraits without rendering adjustments.
      </p>
      <p>
        Three intact Earth images supply different combinations of visible land,
        cloud structure, colour and phase. Each appears at approximately the
        same globe diameter, then at the two flight sizes beside production
        Mercury and Venus. Only uniform scaling and positioning are applied to
        the source images: no recolouring, contrast change, artificial shading,
        new haze, masking or projected map. Source black margins are retained.
      </p>
      <figure>
        <img
          class="comparison"
          src="/studies/earth-2026-11-14/02-2-closure-1.png"
          width="1056"
          height="730"
          alt="Apollo, EPIC and Rosetta references beside Mercury and Venus"
        >
        <figcaption>
          Closure capture · 15 November 2026, 1280px viewport. Existing static
          comparison captured before retiring its renderer; not a new design
          result.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div class="reference-notes">
        <div>
          <h4>A / Apollo 17 · land and weather</h4>
          <p>
            NASA, 7 December 1972, AS17-148-22727. A crew photograph taken en
            route to the Moon, showing Africa, Antarctica and the surrounding
            oceans. The retained NASA web reproduction is a photograph, not a
            stitched global texture. Its processing history is not reconstructed
            here.
          </p>
          <a
            href="https://www.nasa.gov/image-article/apollo-17-astronauts-capture-iconic-blue-marble-50-years-ago/"
            >Image and source context</a
          >.
        </div>
        <div>
          <h4>B / EPIC · weather over the whole globe</h4>
          <p>
            NASA / NOAA, DSCOVR EPIC, 6 July 2015. Red, green and blue
            observations combined into a colour image of the sunlit Earth,
            showing North and Central America. Its clouds and atmospheric
            effects are already in the image; it is not an unlit surface map.
          </p>
          <a
            href="https://www.nasa.gov/image-article/nasa-captures-epic-earth-image/"
            >Image and acquisition context</a
          >.
        </div>
        <div>
          <h4>C / Rosetta · a world mostly in shadow</h4>
          <p>
            ESA, MPS for the OSIRIS Team;
            MPS/UPD/LAM/IAA/RSSD/INTA/UPM/DASP/IDA. Earth’s southern crescent
            during the November 2009 departure, reproduced by NASA APOD on 23
            November. The source’s blue limb, bright weather and dark hemisphere
            are preserved. The cited APOD page does not specify the filter
            combination or complete processing; this is not labelled a
            calibrated true-colour view.
          </p>
          <a
            href="https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2009-november-23-crescent-earth-from-the-departing-rosetta-spacecraft/"
            >Image, credit and context</a
          >.
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        Conditions: enlarged Earth diameter approximately 224px; flight
        diameters 58.8px and 33.6px, using the unchanged r42u comparison fixture
        at 0.7 and 0.4 px/u. Globe bounds are estimated from each visible limb;
        Rosetta’s unlit extent is inferred from its arc. Mercury r27u and Venus
        r40.327526u use their production materials and upper-left light. The
        Earth portraits keep their own original illumination, orientation and
        weather: this is a reference board, not a scene under one Sun. A black
        reference backdrop accommodates the intact source margins.
      </p>
      <p>
        No image or property has been selected. The comparison can identify a
        convincing appearance and qualities that survive reduction; it cannot
        establish motion, relighting, exact material colours or habitation cues.
        These daytime and crescent views do not answer how city lights should
        look. Once a target is established, a reconstruction can be compared
        against it before adding atmospheric or future-world changes.
        <a href="/studies/earth-2026-11-14/references-02-2.json"
          >Source files and display conditions</a
        >.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Retained assessment:</strong>
        these references bring the exploration closer and are useful material to
        build upon. Cloud cover still hides too much of the surface. More
        references are needed, especially cloud-free or sparsely clouded views
        and scientific depictions of future Earth. No appearance is selected.
        The next step expands the research before trying another rendering
        correction or narrowing the design question again.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary id="02-3">
        02.3 / The surface beneath the weather, and possible futures
      </summary>
      <p>
        Seven source images broaden the reference base. The first group exposes
        land, ocean, seasonal ice and habitation; the second shows
        geographically grounded future changes. Open any image for its original
        resolution. Files are displayed intact with no colour adjustments or new
        rendering.
      </p>
      <h4>Cloud-free Earth · observed composites</h4>
      <div
        class="research-grid"
        style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: var(--spacing-lg); margin-block: var(--spacing-lg);"
      >
        <figure>
          <a href="/studies/earth-2026-11-14/january.jpg"
            ><img
              class="comparison"
              src="/studies/earth-2026-11-14/january.jpg"
              alt="Cloud-free global Earth map, January 2004, with extensive northern snow."
              loading="lazy"
            ></a
          >
          <figcaption>
            <strong>January 2004.</strong>
            Blue Marble Next Generation. Snow, desert, vegetation and ocean
            remain visible without the weather layer.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <a href="/studies/earth-2026-11-14/day.jpg"
            ><img
              class="comparison"
              src="/studies/earth-2026-11-14/day.jpg"
              alt="Cloud-free global Earth map, July 2004, showing seasonal differences in snow and vegetation."
              loading="lazy"
            ></a
          >
          <figcaption>
            <strong>July 2004.</strong>
            The same map series and projection. Compare the seasonal
            distribution of snow and vegetation, not a warming forecast.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
      <p>
        NASA Earth Observatory / Reto Stöckli. These monthly composites assemble
        cloud-free observations over time; they are not simultaneous
        photographs. Persistent clouds and distinguishing snow from cloud
        complicate the reconstruction. Their seasonal differences are useful
        surface references, not evidence of climate change between January and
        July.
        <a
          href="https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-observatory/blue-marble-next-generation/"
          >NASA method and monthly series</a
        >.
      </p>
      <div
        class="research-grid"
        style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: var(--spacing-lg); margin-block: var(--spacing-lg);"
      >
        <figure>
          <a href="/studies/earth-2026-11-14/noaa-cloud-free.jpg"
            ><img
              class="comparison"
              src="/studies/earth-2026-11-14/noaa-cloud-free.jpg"
              alt="NOAA Science On a Sphere preview of cloud-free Earth."
              loading="lazy"
            ></a
          >
          <figcaption>
            <strong>A cloud-free globe.</strong>
            NOAA Science On a Sphere preview, using NASA / GSFC Blue Marble
            imagery from 2001. A reference for how visible geography occupies a
            globe. The underlying mosaic combines observations and terrain
            shading; this is a rendered preview, not a cloudless instant
            photographed from space.
            <a
              href="https://sos.noaa.gov/catalog/datasets/blue-marble-without-clouds/"
              >Dataset and method</a
            >.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <a href="/studies/earth-2026-11-14/night.jpg"
            ><img
              class="comparison"
              src="/studies/earth-2026-11-14/night.jpg"
              alt="Black Marble 2016 global night map showing concentrated settlement lights."
              loading="lazy"
            ></a
          >
          <figcaption>
            <strong>Inhabitation at night · 2016.</strong>
            Black Marble, NASA Earth Observatory / Joshua Stevens, Suomi NPP
            VIIRS data from Miguel Román, NASA GSFC. A processed global
            composite: settlement patterns rather than a uniformly glowing
            landmass. Historical lights do not predict settlement in 2300.
            <a
              href="https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-observatory/earth-at-night/maps/"
              >Source and processing</a
            >.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
      <h4>Future Earth · model visualizations</h4>
      <figure>
        <a href="/studies/earth-2026-11-14/greenland-2300.jpg"
          ><img
            class="comparison"
            src="/studies/earth-2026-11-14/greenland-2300.jpg"
            width="1024"
            height="576"
            alt="Northeastern Greenland in 2300 under low, medium and high emissions scenarios, left to right."
            loading="lazy"
          ></a
        >
        <figcaption>
          <strong>Northeastern Greenland · 2300.</strong>
          Low, medium and high emissions scenarios, left to right. Violet
          identifies bed exposed since 2008; the colours encode scientific
          information, not predicted ground appearance. NASA Scientific
          Visualization Studio, Cindy Starr, released 2020; Parallel Ice Sheet
          Model simulations.
          <a href="https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/4804/"
            >Three Futures: animation, model context and credits</a
          >.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        This provides a concrete future geography reference within the proposed
        time range. It can inform where ice retreats and land becomes exposed.
        It does not supply a true-colour future surface, a global coastline or a
        preferred scenario for the game.
      </p>
      <div
        class="research-grid"
        style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: var(--spacing-lg); margin-block: var(--spacing-lg);"
      >
        <figure>
          <a href="/studies/earth-2026-11-14/land-use-1900.png"
            ><img
              class="comparison"
              src="/studies/earth-2026-11-14/land-use-1900.png"
              alt="Historical primary land cover map for 1900 with its original legend."
              loading="lazy"
            ></a
          >
          <figcaption>
            <strong>Land use · 1900 historical baseline.</strong>
            Primary vegetation means vegetation never disturbed by activities
            such as farming or wood harvesting.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <a href="/studies/earth-2026-11-14/land-use-2100.png"
            ><img
              class="comparison"
              src="/studies/earth-2026-11-14/land-use-2100.png"
              alt="Projected primary land cover in 2100 under the AIM medium-high emissions pathway, with original legend."
              loading="lazy"
            ></a
          >
          <figcaption>
            <strong>Land use · 2100 projection.</strong>
            AIM model, medium-high emissions pathway. Colour represents the
            proportion of primary land cover, not the colour a satellite would
            photograph.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
      <p>
        NASA ORNL DAAC; Chini, Hurtt and Frolking, Harmonized Global Land Use,
        2014, approximately 50 km resolution. These maps concern human land use,
        not climate alone. Recovered secondary vegetation is excluded from
        “primary,” so low values do not mean bare ground. This helps research an
        inhabited future without inventing cities from a colour map.
        <a
          href="https://mynasadata.larc.nasa.gov/mini-lessonactivity/comparing-global-land-use-over-time"
          >NASA explanation and dataset citation</a
        >.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>What remains missing:</strong>
        this research found regional and thematic projections, rather than a
        validated, cloud-free, true-colour portrait of the entire Earth in
        2100–2400. A future globe would still require authored interpretation of
        a chosen scenario. No palette, atmosphere, climate scenario or
        appearance target is selected here.
        <a href="/studies/earth-2026-11-14/references-02-3.json"
          >New asset provenance</a
        >.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Retained lesson:</strong>
        02.3 was a valuable step because it exposed a gap between scientifically
        grounded imagery and a convincing feeling of Earth. Some ocean
        treatments read as deep-blue plastic or a children’s toy; less saturated
        photographic references are preferred. The cause remains unresolved. It
        may reach back to the initial design question, but that connection is
        tentative. This is a lesson from the exploration, not an isolated fault
        or a new procedure. It does not establish that desaturation alone would
        resolve the appearance.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary id="02-4">02.4 / Different ways of seeing Earth</summary>
      <p>
        Six references extend the research across crew photography, another
        satellite provider, spacecraft colour imaging and authored 3D views.
        Scientific sources remain useful for geography; their appearance need
        not prescribe the game’s surface. These are examples to look at, not
        candidates ranked by accuracy or a request to choose the final renderer.
      </p>
      <p>
        Original framing, colour, weather and exposure are retained. Open an
        image to inspect the source file. Different viewpoints and presentation
        sizes prevent a controlled comparison: a regional photograph can suggest
        water or depth without showing how a whole globe will read at flight
        scale.
      </p>
      <div
        class="research-grid"
        style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: var(--spacing-lg); margin-block: var(--spacing-lg);"
      >
        <figure style="margin: 0;">
          <a href="/studies/earth-2026-11-14/earthrise.jpg"
            ><img
              class="comparison"
              style="width: 100%; height: 340px; object-fit: contain;"
              src="/studies/earth-2026-11-14/earthrise.jpg"
              alt="A / Earthrise · crew photography"
              loading="lazy"
            ></a
          >
          <figcaption>
            <strong>A / Earthrise · crew photography.</strong>
            Bill Anders / NASA, Apollo 8, 24 December 1968. The pale Earth and
            surrounding black space offer a different impression from a surface
            map. The lunar foreground and small globe contribute to that
            impression; neither is a proposed game composition.
            <a
              href="https://science.nasa.gov/resource/apollo-8s-iconic-earthrise/"
              >NASA photograph and credit</a
            >.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure style="margin: 0;">
          <a href="/studies/earth-2026-11-14/iss-sunglint.jpg"
            ><img
              class="comparison"
              style="width: 100%; height: 340px; object-fit: contain;"
              src="/studies/earth-2026-11-14/iss-sunglint.jpg"
              alt="B / Mediterranean sunglint · crew photography"
              loading="lazy"
            ></a
          >
          <figcaption>
            <strong>B / Mediterranean sunglint · crew photography.</strong>
            NASA / ISS, frame ISS062-E-44814. Corsica and Sardinia sit against
            bright reflected sunlight. Water can read as silver and haze as
            depth, rather than relying on deep blue. This is a local,
            angle-dependent appearance, not a palette for the entire ocean.
            Original camera orientation retained.
            <a
              href="https://eol.jsc.nasa.gov/Collections/EarthFromSpace/caption.pl?PHOTO=ISS062-E-44814"
              >NASA frame explanation</a
            >.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
      <div
        class="research-grid"
        style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: var(--spacing-lg); margin-block: var(--spacing-lg);"
      >
        <figure style="margin: 0;">
          <a href="/studies/earth-2026-11-14/meteosat-2023.jpg"
            ><img
              class="comparison"
              style="width: 100%; height: 340px; object-fit: contain;"
              src="/studies/earth-2026-11-14/meteosat-2023.jpg"
              alt="C / Meteosat · another satellite view"
              loading="lazy"
            ></a
          >
          <figcaption>
            <strong>C / Meteosat · another satellite view.</strong>
            EUMETSAT, MTG-I1 Flexible Combined Imager, 18 March 2023, 11:50 UTC.
            True-colour processed imagery of Europe, Africa and the Atlantic.
            Muted land, dark water and a bright reflection supply a different
            balance from the earlier cloud-free globe. Clouds remain part of
            this reference.
            <a
              href="https://www.eumetsat.int/features/discover-first-images-mtg-i1"
              >EUMETSAT acquisition and processing context</a
            >.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure style="margin: 0;">
          <a href="/studies/earth-2026-11-14/rosetta-2007.webp"
            ><img
              class="comparison"
              style="width: 100%; height: 340px; object-fit: contain;"
              src="/studies/earth-2026-11-14/rosetta-2007.webp"
              alt="D / Rosetta · spacecraft colour composite"
              loading="lazy"
            ></a
          >
          <figcaption>
            <strong>D / Rosetta · spacecraft colour composite.</strong>
            ESA, MPS for OSIRIS Team; MPS/UPD/LAM/IAA/RSSD/INTA/UPM/DASP/IDA.
