    <details>
      <summary id="02-8">
        02.8 / What makes this planet feel like Earth?
      </summary>
      <p>
        The references and sketches have brought us closer to an Earth that
        could belong in Void Drift. They have also changed how we understand the
        task. We began with actual Earth imagery as the material, but a faithful
        image did not necessarily feel convincing in the game. The ocean could
        look plastic, and adding atmosphere could not resolve a surface that
        felt off. The question now concerns how these parts make a world
        together.
      </p>
      <h4>What changed through the exploration</h4>
      <p>
        In <a href="#02-1">02.1–02.2</a>, the surface made it difficult to judge
        whether the atmosphere belonged. Increasing colour intensity did not
        help.
        <a href="#02-3">02.3</a>
        made another difficulty visible: even photographic Earth references
        could have a blue that felt artificial. Scientific provenance alone
        could not tell us which appearance would work.
      </p>
      <p>
        The less saturated examples in <a href="#02-4">02.4</a> suggested an
        “analog photographic” character. The brighter Natural Earth maps in
        <a href="#02-5">02.5</a>
        offered a different kind of value: graphic shapes and more detailed
        seas. All nine authored variations in
        <a href="#02-6">02.6</a>
        were a substantial improvement over the originals. No single variation
        was chosen, but together they showed that interpreting the references
        could bring us closer to the desired feeling.
      </p>
      <p>
        The game examples in <a href="#02-7">02.7</a> opened up the possibility
        that simpler shapes could carry the planet’s identity. The collection is
        too narrow to draw conclusions about a whole era of games. For this
        study, it leaves a useful question: how much familiar geography does our
        Earth need at flight scale, and what detail can give way to a clearer
        composition?
      </p>
      <h4>Reading the planet as a whole</h4>
      <p>
        Earth’s identity may depend on a few familiar land shapes and the
        relationships between land, water, clouds and air. That would leave room
        to alter proportions and simplify detail while keeping it recognizable.
        The evidence gives us a reason to explore this, but has not yet shown
        how far we can go. The restrained colour preference remains useful; it
        does not settle the shapes or the degree of simplification.
      </p>
      <p>
        Separating the ground from clouds and atmosphere is a promising way to
        work on those relationships. It could let the surface retain its
        identity while weather and air give it depth. The earlier difficulty
        still matters: the atmosphere’s fit has to be judged against a
        convincing body. The successful 02.6 images combine these parts in one
        image, so they do not yet show whether separating them will preserve
        what worked.
      </p>
      <p>
        Habitation adds another question. The night side needs some sign of
        life; lights are the clearest possibility raised so far. How can they
        suggest people living on this world at the scale of play? A separate
        treatment for lights, alongside surface, weather and atmospheric light,
        may help. The proposed four-layer arrangement is a way to think about
        the parts, with its construction and final appearance still unresolved.
      </p>
      <h4>What this changes in 01</h4>
      <p>
        <strong>Scope amendment · 15 November 2026.</strong>
        Earth is the first prototype for an Earth-like design language. It needs
        to read as Earth in the arcade sense, with Void Drift’s visual language
        shaping the depiction. The earlier emphasis on actual imagery and
        preserved continental proportions had pulled the question towards
        accuracy. That may help explain some of the exploration difficulties,
        although this sequence cannot isolate their cause. The two questions in
        <a href="#01">01</a>
        now retain both the broader purpose and the concrete Earth prototype.
      </p>
      <p>
        We have enough references to turn our attention to composition. What
        remains open is how much simplification preserves Earth’s identity, what
        makes its atmosphere belong, and how habitation reads after sunset.
        Climate around 2100–2400 and restrained technology remain possible
        influences. The intended outcome is still one Earth in Practice; other
        Earth-like worlds can draw on what we learn here.
      </p>
    </details>
    <h3 id="02-9">02.9 / How much shape does Earth need?</h3>
    <p>
      A familiar coastline may carry more identity than a field of terrain
      detail. These sketches explore that possibility using H1 / Silver from
      02.6 as a common starting point. It is an anchor for this comparison, not
      a chosen winner from the earlier nine.
    </p>
    <p>
      A retains the original detail. B softens terrain into broader painted
      regions while keeping much of the coastline. C redraws the continents more
      boldly, with larger openings and fewer small shapes. The question is
      whether that economy helps Earth read in the game, or takes away too much
      of its character as a world.
    </p>
    <h4>Finding · useful inspiration, unresolved design</h4>
    <p>
      None of the examples through 02.9 yet conveys an inhabited planet with an
      atmosphere that belongs in Void Drift. The reference gathering and
      sketches have been valuable, but improvement between isolated images has
      not established progress towards that whole-game appearance. This does not
      invalidate the earlier preferences or the learning from them.
    </p>
    <p>
      02.8 helped expose the gap; 02.9 returned to adjusting a globe’s surface.
      Putting that globe beside Mercury and Venus did not answer how it belongs
      in the game as a whole. The comparison therefore remains here as evidence
      of that limit, with no variant selected. Clouds and atmosphere baked into
      the portraits also keep their relationship to the surface largely outside
      the exploration.
    </p>
    <p>
      The unresolved issue is the overall conception of an inhabited world in
      Void Drift. Rendering choices have occupied the work before that
      conception is clear enough to give them purpose. Further exploration may
      take many steps; the number is not the concern. What needs to change is
      what those steps help us understand. No next rendering treatment follows
      from this finding.
    </p>
